# gpu-jax-g4dn-2b — authoritative configuration.
#
# The directory name is documentation; this file decides. Never copy a slot value
# out of the directory name into a CLI flag (NAMING.md, "The name is a claim").
#
# Committed on purpose and deliberately NOT gitignored. A real environment
# variable always wins over it: mcp-run.sh exports only unset keys, Makefiles
# use ?=, and dotenv does not overwrite.
#
# STATUS: THIS RIG HAS SERVED NOTHING. Forked from `gpu-jax-g5g-2b` 2026-08-28 to
# put the SAME engine on the SAME GPU generation with the host architecture
# changed -- Intel/AMD x86_64 instead of Graviton2 aarch64. `benchmarks/` is
# deliberately empty.
#
# EVERY value below that is marked MEASURED was measured on G5g (T4G), NOT here.
# The T4 and the T4G are both Turing SM 7.5, so chip-level values should carry;
# anything host-dependent (install time, load staging, host RSS) should not be
# assumed. Do not quote any number in this file as a result for this rig.
#
# WHY THIS RIG EXISTS: it is the A/B control that separates Turing from
# Graviton2. `gpu-jax-g5g-2b` measured 13.10 tok/s and 0.0% TensorCore
# utilisation, and nothing in that run can tell you how much of it is the chip
# and how much is the host. This rig changes exactly one variable.

# --- Model -------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reference instruction-tuned release, so the rig name carries no encoding
# slot. MODELS.md: 10.2 GB / 9.5 GiB of bf16 weights, 18 KiB/token of KV.
#
# Deliberately NOT the -qat-w4a16-ct export the TPU JAX rig serves. Two reasons,
# and the second is the load-bearing one:
#   1. The dense model fits: 9.5 GiB of weights in 15360 MiB of device memory.
#   2. The fused W4A16 Pallas kernel CANNOT run on Turing. On GPU, Pallas lowers
#      through Triton and its tiles become shared memory, which Turing caps at
#      64 KiB per block; the kernel needs 550 KiB - 1.1 MiB at this model's
#      shapes. check_w4a16_fits_scoped_memory() in ports/gemma4/jax_e_model.py
#      computes this, but it is only reached from the FUSED path and the engine
#      defaults to w4a16_impl="reference", so it never fires. MEASURED 2026-08-23:
#      w4a16 loads here, and weights really do pack down -- 6.56 GB, not dense.
#
#      What actually blocks w4a16 alone is TRANSIENT, not resident: every request
#      dies allocating 4.52 GiB beside 6.56 GB of resident weights, so all of them
#      500. That allocation is NOT identified -- it is NOT a dequantised Linear
#      (the largest one measures 0.005 GB dequantised). The only tensor near that
#      size is the PLE table, /embed_tokens_per_layer (262144, 8960) bf16 =
#      4.698 GB, which the QAT export leaves unquantised -- close to 4.52 GiB but
#      not equal to it, so treat the cause as open. Adding
#      PLE_BITS=4 frees the headroom and the pair works: 3.05 GB, 13.5 tok/s.
#      To serve QAT here set BOTH, never w4a16 on its own:
#        MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it-qat-w4a16-ct
#        QUANT_MODE=auto
#        PLE_BITS=4
MODEL_NAME=google/gemma-4-E2B-it

# auto reads the mode off the checkpoint name (w4a16 if the name says so, else
# fp16). Pinned to fp16 here because the checkpoint above is dense.
QUANT_MODE=fp16

# --- Hardware ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The hardware slot is `g4dn` — the EC2 instance family. Slot 3's normal rule is
# the GPU SKU, and here the two agree closely enough that no carve-out is
# needed: the GPU is an NVIDIA T4 and `t4` is not an EC2 instance family. The
# family name is still the better slot value, because it also pins the x86_64
# host. (Contrast the sibling `gpu-jax-g5g-2b`, which NEEDS its carve-out: `t4g`
# collides with the t4g.* burstable CPU instances. NAMING.md has both.)
INSTANCE_TYPE=g4dn.xlarge
AWS_REGION=us-east-1

# Read from describe_instance_types 2026-08-28, not from a product page. The GPU
# count is NOT monotonic in the size suffix — 12xlarge has four T4s and 16xlarge
# has one — so never infer it from the name.
#
#   size            GPUs  GPU mem  vCPU   RAM
#   g4dn.xlarge      1     16 GB     4   16 GB   <- default
#   g4dn.2xlarge     1     16 GB     8   32 GB
#   g4dn.4xlarge     1     16 GB    16   64 GB
#   g4dn.8xlarge     1     16 GB    32  128 GB
#   g4dn.12xlarge    4     64 GB    48  192 GB
#   g4dn.16xlarge    1     16 GB    64  256 GB
#   g4dn.metal       8    128 GB    96  384 GB
#
# EVERY size is supported: _validate_instance_type enforces only the size list,
# and _user_data provisions a swapfile at or below 16 GiB of host RAM — which on
# g4dn is the default xlarge alone. Every other size has >= 32 GiB.

# Nothing shards across GPUs. The JAX engine is single-device
# (jax.devices()[0]), _serve_argv emits no tensor-parallel flag, and the payload
# contains no sharding primitives at all, so on 12xlarge/metal the extra GPUs
# idle. A bigger instance buys host RAM and network, NOT device memory: the
# per-GPU budget is the same 15360 MiB on every size.
TENSOR_PARALLEL_SIZE=1

# --- Turing (SM 7.5) constraints ---------------------------------------------
# MEASURED on hardware 2026-08-12 by the sibling vLLM rig. These are properties
# of the chip, so they hold here too.
#
# The T4 is Turing and has no bf16 datapath. It is not a hard failure: XLA accepts
# bfloat16 and emulates it through fp32 conversions, so the numbers come out
# right while every matmul quietly pays a conversion. float16 is what actually
# executes on Turing tensor cores.
#
# jax_e_model.py decides this from the live device's compute capability rather
# than from this file — anything below SM 8.0 gets float16. The value here is
# the override, and setting it to bfloat16 would emulate rather than fail.
DTYPE=float16

# Turing has no fp8 either, so the fp8 KV cache the L4 rigs enable is
# unavailable; resolve_cache_dtype() raises rather than silently downgrading.
# `auto` follows the compute dtype, so this resolves to float16 on a T4.
# int8 is the supported way to halve KV here — it carries a per-row scale.
KV_CACHE_DTYPE=auto

# MEASURED ON THIS RIG 2026-08-29: 15360 MiB total on a Tesla T4, not the
# nominal 16 GB — the same figure the G5g sibling measured for the T4G, which is
# the first direct confirmation that the two chips present an identical budget.
#
# This is JAX's preallocation knob, not vLLM's GPU_MEMORY_UTILIZATION — they are
# not the same thing and the number is not transferable. JAX preallocates this
# fraction of device memory at first use; what is left over is NOT a KV pool
# managed by an engine, because this engine allocates its KV cache as ordinary
# JAX arrays inside that fraction.
XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_MEM_FRACTION=0.90

# MEASURED 2026-08-23: KV is NOT the binding constraint, prefill is. But the
# numbers below are BRACKETS, not thresholds -- only these prompt sizes were
# tried, so the real ceiling sits somewhere inside each interval:
#
#   dense fp16         served 115, FAILED at 2,015  -> ceiling in (115, 2015]
#   QAT w4a16 + ple4   served 3,515, FAILED at 5,015 -> ceiling in (3515, 5015]
#
# The engine's ~2.13 MB/token prefill figure predicts 2,262 and 5,174. Both are
# ABOVE the measured failure points, i.e. it OVER-predicts capacity, in the
# unsafe direction -- and it was measured on v6e-1, not on a T4G. Do not size a
# deployment from it. The dense bracket in particular is 17x wide and gives
# almost no resolution.
#
# Either way 8192 is NOT servable one-shot on either config, and this value is a
# KV allocation size, not a promise about prompt length. Set PREFILL_CHUNK_SIZE
# to bound the prefill pass independently of it.
#
# Separately and NOT an OOM: the quantised config emitted degenerate output
# ("TheTheThe...") at 3,515 prompt tokens, well inside its own budget, and the
# server counted it status="success". Cause unresolved.
#
# LOWERED 8192 -> 4096 on 2026-08-26, because 8192 was not reachable. MEASURED:
# a 4,105-token prompt serves; 5,120 fails with RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED allocating
# 2.59 GiB, and the failing allocation grows ~0.9 MiB/token from there (3.48 GiB
# at 6,144; 5.11 GiB at 7,800). Advertising 8192 was a trap for anything sizing
# against it. The documented mitigation is NOT available: PREFILL_CHUNK_SIZE
# raises unless window_kv=False, and window_kv auto-resolves True whenever
# max_model_len > sliding_window (512). Raise this again only once prefill
# temporaries are actually bounded.
MAX_MODEL_LEN=4096
MAX_NUM_SEQS=1

# Quantisation knobs, reachable from the rig as of 2026-08-23 (_serve_argv emits
# them). PLE_BITS quantises the per-layer-embedding table -- a gather, never a
# matmul, so dequantising fetched rows is nearly free. 0 = off, else 4 or 8.
# Measured saving at 4 bits: 3.505 GB (the table is /embed_tokens_per_layer,
# (262144, 8960) bf16 = 4.698 GB, read off the loaded model on 2026-08-23).
#
# CHANGED on 2026-08-26 from PLE_BITS=0 / INT8_LM_HEAD= after the first
# measurement of these levers (benchmarks/runs/2026-08-26-quant-levers-fixed-g5g).
# ple4+int8head is strictly better than the old default on BOTH axes:
#   weights 9.257 GB -> 6.155 GB (-33.5%)   decode 12.80 -> 13.10 tok/s (+2.3%)
# Both source-comment predictions held to within 1%, and int8_lm_head's cost is
# exactly 262144x1536x1 B = +0.403 GB.
#
# INT8_LM_HEAD IS NOT NUMERICS-PRESERVING (~0.8% logit error). It is the only
# lever here that trades accuracy, and the only one that moves throughput at
# all -- set it to empty to get the numerics-preserving configuration back.
# Note it does NOT do an int8 matmul: it dequantises the whole table to fp16
# every decode step, which is why the win is 2% and not more.
PLE_BITS=4
INT8_LM_HEAD=1

# Empty = one-shot prefill (previous behaviour). Set to a token count to bound
# prefill temporaries; see the MAX_MODEL_LEN note above for the arithmetic.
PREFILL_CHUNK_SIZE=

# --- Serving -----------------------------------------------------------------
JAX_PORT=8000
SERVICE_NAME=jax-g4dn
HF_SECRET_ID=vllm/hf-token

# --- JAX runtime -------------------------------------------------------------
# The whole reason this rig exists. The vLLM path on this hardware needs a
# from-source build for SM 7.5 (~67 min) plus a CUDA toolkit, plus Rust, plus an
# unlanded patch to Triton's attention kernel. JAX needs none of it:
#
#   * jaxlib and both CUDA plugin/pjrt packages publish manylinux x86_64
#     wheels. This is much WEAKER evidence than the same sentence was on the
#     G5g sibling, and should not be quoted as a finding: that rig ran on
#     aarch64, where a missing wheel was a real and recurring hazard. x86_64 is
#     the architecture every one of these projects publishes first.
#   * The plugin's arch tables contain sm_75 and its floor is SM 6.0
#     ("device not supported (request SM60+)"). VERIFIED by inspecting
#     jax_cuda12_pjrt 0.11.1's xla_cuda_plugin.so.
#   * Every CUDA dependency (cublas, cudnn, cuda-runtime, cusolver, nvcc)
#     publishes x86_64 wheels, so pip supplies CUDA and the DLAMI only has to
#     supply the driver.
#
# The load-bearing claim is the SECOND one -- sm_75 -- because that is about the
# chip, and the chip is what the vLLM path cannot handle without a build. The
# architecture claims carry no weight here.
#
# That list is about installability, which is not the same as "it serves". The
# serving question is settled independently: MEASURED ON THIS RIG 2026-08-29,
# see benchmarks/runs/2026-08-29-first-serve-g4dn/.
# cuda13, not cuda12. The DLAMI driver is 580.126.09 and nvidia-smi reports
# CUDA Version: 13.0, so the newer line is supported by the driver we get.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-21 on i-08639f402a3c3e76b -- installed, booted and served,
# not merely resolved. Every version the 2026-08-20 `pip install --dry-run`
# predicted came true exactly: nvidia-cublas 13.6.1.10, nvidia-cuda-runtime
# 13.3.29, nvidia-cuda-nvcc 13.3.73, alongside jax/jaxlib 0.11.1 and
# jax-cuda13-{pjrt,plugin} 0.11.1 against driver 580.126.09.
#
# verify_gpu_arch reported compute capability 7.5 and `fp16 matmul ok: True`, and
# the endpoint then served at 12.4 tok/s decode -- indistinguishable from the
# 12.5 tok/s the CUDA 12 / Python 3.12 stack recorded on 2026-08-19. This bump
# buys currency, NOT speed; do not cite it as a performance change.
#
# GOTCHA: NVIDIA dropped the -cuXX suffix for the CUDA 13 line, so the real
# packages are `nvidia-cublas`, `nvidia-cuda-runtime`, `nvidia-cusolver`.
# `nvidia-cublas-cu13` still exists on PyPI as a 0.0.1 stub reading
# "DEPRECATED: Use nvidia-cublas instead" -- which looks exactly like "CUDA 13 is
# unpublished" if you check the suffixed name and stop there. cudnn
# and nccl are the exceptions and kept theirs (nvidia-cudnn-cu13).
JAX_PIP_SPEC=jax[cuda13]

# 3.14, the newest stable CPython. NOT 3.12, whose bugfix window closed
# 2025-04-02 -- it has been security-only for over a year, and an interpreter off
# bugfix support is exactly the pin that turns into debugging time later.
# NOT 3.15 either: deadsnakes offers only 3.15.0~rc1, and a release candidate
# buys the same risk this preference exists to avoid. (jaxlib does publish
# cp315 wheels — that is jaxlib building against a pre-release, not evidence
# that 3.15 is out.) Ubuntu 26.04 ships 3.14 as its system python, so the newest
# stable interpreter and the distro's own now agree.
#
# Every dependency resolves at cp314 on x86_64 — CONFIRMED 2026-08-29 by an
# install that completed in 76s and then passed the GPU assertion. Note that
# safetensors/tokenizers ship cp310-abi3, which is forward-compatible; they LOOK
# cp310-only in a naive scan of the wheel tags.
JAX_PYTHON_VERSION=3.14

# Persistent XLA compilation cache. Skips recompilation on restart.
#
# THIS VARIABLE DID NOTHING UNTIL 2026-08-27, and the old comment here ("~17s
# saved on the TPU rig; unmeasured") described an effect this rig was not
# getting. ports/gemma4/jax_e_model.py set the cache dir unconditionally at
# import, and jax_openai_server imports it AFTER resolving this variable, so the
# port silently won every start. MEASURED on i-021f15b2b45e13793: the process
# had JAX_COMPILATION_CACHE_DIR=/opt/jax-cache in its environment and that
# directory held ZERO files, while 447 files accumulated under the port's
# hardcoded ~/.cache/jax_compilation_cache. Fixed by making the port honour it.
#
# NOW MEASURED here rather than inherited from the TPU rig. Restoring a
# 413-file cache before start, against an empty one, on the same request shape:
# see benchmarks/runs/2026-08-27-ubuntu2604-base-g5g/ for the A/B.
JAX_COMPILATION_CACHE_DIR=/opt/jax-cache

# Optional S3 backing for that cache, so it survives a relaunch rather than
# dying with the instance. EMPTY = disabled, which is the shipped default.
# The instance profile must already allow s3 on the prefix; this rig does not
# create buckets or widen IAM. Upload is on a timer, not a shutdown hook,
# because spot gives ~2 minutes and does not reliably run ExecStopPost.
JAX_CACHE_S3_URI=
JAX_CACHE_SYNC_MINUTES=10

# --- AMI ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# x86_64 GPU DLAMI. Two independent requirements:
#   1. x86_64 — enforced by the Architecture filter in _resolve_ami's fallback,
#      and by the /x86_64/ segment of the SSM path below.
#   2. NVIDIA driver — AWS ships plenty of DLAMIs without one. Such an image
#      boots fine on a G4dn and simply has no GPU visible to JAX, which reads as
#      a broken runtime rather than a wrong AMI. The bootstrap's verify_gpu step
#      is what turns that into an install-time failure instead of a serve-time
#      one.
# The SSM public parameter pins both, so it is preferred over a name filter.
#
# Do not hardcode an AMI id even though this rig is x86_64 and the legacy
# tips-tree pin (ami-012ba162b9cd2729c) would now at least boot: it is a frozen
# image from a dead line, and rot is the actual hazard here, not architecture.
#
# Unlike the vLLM rig there is no prebuilt AMI here and none is needed: the
# install is a pip install, not a compile, so a stock DLAMI is the right base.
#
# CHANGED 2026-08-27, from
#   .../oss-nvidia-driver-gpu-pytorch-2.7-ubuntu-22.04/latest/ami-id
# for two independent reasons. NOT YET LAUNCHED — see the warning below.
#
#   * BASE, not PyTorch. This rig never installs into the DLAMI's PyTorch (it
#     ships its own CUDA libraries and jax brings its own), so a PyTorch DLAMI
#     is GBs of image whose entire content is deliberately unused.
#   * `/latest/` is only the latest build WITHIN a PyTorch+Ubuntu line, and AWS
#     freezes those lines. The old parameter resolved to ami-077792d0bb6a000b8,
#     built 2026-05-02, and could never move again — AWS stopped rebuilding
#     22.04 after PyTorch 2.7. It READ as "track latest" and was in fact a pin
#     to a dead line. VERIFIED 2026-08-27 by enumerating the DLAMI parameters:
#     22.04 stops at pytorch-2.7, and 2.8–2.12 are 24.04 only. The finding is
#     about how AWS freezes these lines, so it applies to the x86_64 tree too.
#
# 26.04 rather than 24.04 because Ubuntu 26.04 ships **Python 3.14.3 as the
# system interpreter** (VERIFIED 2026-08-27 in the Ubuntu archive), which is
# exactly JAX_PYTHON_VERSION — so the deadsnakes PPA leaves the critical path.
# 24.04 ships 3.12 and would still need it. deadsnakes publishes python3.14 for
# jammy and noble only, and does not need to for resolute.
#
# VERIFIED ON HARDWARE 2026-08-27 on i-021f15b2b45e13793 (ami-0bff4343bfd56a20e).
# All three risks settled, and the third was real rather than defensive:
#   * driver 595.91.07 (was 580.126.09) — well past jax[cuda13]'s 580+ floor;
#   * aws-cli/2.36.30 IS present, and HF_TOKEN landed in the EnvironmentFile;
#   * /usr/lib/python3.14/EXTERNALLY-MANAGED EXISTS, so PEP 668 is genuinely in
#     force and --break-system-packages is load-bearing — without it the first
#     pip install would have failed outright.
# Also measured: glibc 2.43 (was 2.35, exactly on xprof's wheel floor), Python
# 3.14.4 at /usr/bin/python3.14 so deadsnakes is skipped, and an INSTALL of
# ~80 SECONDS against the >21 minutes that a spot reclamation cut short on
# 2026-08-25. See gpu-jax-g5g-2b's benchmarks/runs/2026-08-27-ubuntu2604-base-g5g/.
#
# CONFIRMED ON THIS RIG 2026-08-29 (i-02eb43cb99429a8f7, ami-0216c4aa131462acf):
# the x86_64 26.04 base behaves the same way. Install 76s TOTAL, INCLUDING xprof
# and tensorboard, python3.14 already present so deadsnakes was skipped, and
# `jax 0.11.1 devices: [CudaDevice(id=0)] compute_capability: 7.5`.
# To fall back, set this to the 24.04 base (deadsnakes still runs) or to the
# old 22.04 line above.
DLAMI_SSM_PARAMETER=/aws/service/deeplearning/ami/x86_64/base-oss-nvidia-driver-gpu-ubuntu-26.04/latest/ami-id

# Fallback for describe-images. Requires the driver in the name so a driverless
# image cannot match, and deliberately names no architecture — _resolve_ami
# already filters Architecture=x86_64, and the base images are named
# "Deep Learning Base OSS Nvidia Driver GPU AMI (Ubuntu 26.04)".
#
# THIS KEY AND DLAMI_SSM_PARAMETER MUST MOVE TOGETHER. On the G5g sibling they
# did not: the SSM path moved to the base image while the name filter still
# required "Deep Learning ARM64 AMI" contiguously, so the fallback would have
# quietly resolved the OLD PyTorch image — a revert that reads as a success.
# test_tpu_env_agrees_with_server_defaults now covers both keys.
DLAMI_NAME=Deep Learning Base OSS Nvidia Driver GPU*Ubuntu*
